--- body ---
## Context

Author, 2026-09-06: "SDT ca dit rien, 'Index 43%' on comprend" -- SDT means
nothing to a reader, "Index 43%" is immediately understood. Ticket 0686
already asked for "human-readable states" and to "move technical
diagnostics below primary progress information"; this ticket supplies the
actual wording 0686 should adopt, so the two are not solved twice with
different vocabularies.

## Vocabulary

| Internal | User-facing (en) | fr |
|---|---|---|
| SDT pack sitter | Index assistant | Assistant d'indexation |
| SDT pack | index (of a file) | index (du fichier) |
| coverage | Index 43 % | Index 43 % |
| native full-text index (Zotero's own) | text search index | index de recherche textuelle |
| attachment (the unit of work) | file | fichier |
| item owning the attachment | reference | reference |
| active attachment | Indexing: <reference> - <file> | Indexation : <reference> - <fichier> |
| finished with exceptions | 12 files could not be indexed | 12 fichiers n'ont pas pu etre indexes |

"SDT", "pack", "processor", "fingerprint" survive only inside the
diagnostics disclosure (see 0693), where a reader has explicitly asked for
them.

Banned outright from the UI, alongside "SDT" and "pack": **"document",
"element", "piece jointe", "item"**. The sitter's unit of work is one
attachment -- it lists `itemAttachments` and calls `Zotero.SDT.ensure` on an
attachment id -- while a reference can carry several attachments (SPEC.md D6,
twin-attachment rule), so "document" collapses two things the UI must keep
apart. Worse, Zotero's own French UI already translates *item* as "document",
so to a French reader the word names the reference, which is the opposite of
what it would mean here. "Document" also already carries two unrelated senses
in this repo (README's chapter-sized content unit; SPEC.md's retrieval unit,
deliberately distinguished from "entry") -- four collisions on one word.

Zotero auto-names attachments ("Full Text PDF", "Snapshot"), so the
attachment title alone identifies nothing. Any line naming the active work
leads with the reference title and appends the file.

One naming conflict to resolve, not just translate around: Zotero already
has its own "full-text index" panel. The sitter's object keeps the word
"index"; Zotero's older one is relabeled "text search index" and confined
to the disclosure, so the two are never both called "index" in view at
once.

## Acceptance

- [x] Toolbar, dialog, and all user-facing prompts use only the
      user-facing column above; "SDT" does not appear outside the
      diagnostics disclosure. (PR #369. manifest.json's name and description
      are out of scope: Add-ons Manager, not toolbar/dialog/prompt.)
- [x] The two "index" concepts are never both visible under the same
      unqualified label in one view. (PR #369, same commit as the sitter's
      own rename.)
- [x] Every count names the unit it counts (files). The active line shows the
      reference title when one exists, never the attachment's generic
      auto-generated title alone. (PR #369; `describeSDTFile` composes
      reference then file for both the progress line and the error line.)
- [x] A blocked or failed sitter is distinguishable from a healthy idle one
      without opening the dialog, and no internal phase name reaches the
      toolbar to do it. (PR #369; SDT_PHASE_LABELS, with a test enumerating
      the phase literals so a new one cannot ship untranslated.)
- [ ] Wording lands before 0692 (i18n) externalizes it -- do not bundle a
      wording change and a string-externalization change in one PR; they
      cannot be diffed against each other. (Half met: PR #369 carries no
      externalization. The ordering half is only observable at merge, so this
      box stays open until 0692 lands after it.)
